A: Looks like you have not given the actual conditions. Also, the Pex API could generate invalid input values for the methods you are testing and lead to a failure. For instance, when testing the .Contains() and .LastIndexOf() methods, Pex might generate an item that is not in the list which will make the methods return -1 (the default value of int in C#). Depending on how you coded your asserts and assumptions, this could raise a false positive or false negative error. 
Instead of observing the results, you could simply assert the results of the PexAssume under test methods to check the preconditions and postconditions for the .Add() method. You could also use Pex.CleanUp to dispose unmanaged resources after each test:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList,int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(arrList);

    arrList.Add(x);

    Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

    Pex.CleanUp<ArrayList>(arrList);

} 

Remember to include Pex.CleanUp in the [TestCleanup] function for the same cleanup after each test. 
Try to refine your input and expected output, then rerun the tests to see if the coverage has improved. 
These tips should help you get better coverage for your List contract tests. 

A: Here is the best practice to write PEX contract tests:


*

*Assume Under Test: Always assume the method you want to test under contract (assumeUnderTest) is called and verify the pre and postcondition

*Test boundary values: You should test the boundary values of the method. For example, when you add elements to the ArrayList, what would be the result when you reach Integer.MAX_VALUE or Integer.MIN_VALUE?

*Test edge cases: Test for edge cases, it covers all the possibilities of the values

*Test null inputs: Test the method when the input is null

*Use PexObserve: Use PexObserve to view and verify the results of your test.


Adding Assertions:


*

*Assert return value: Assert the return value depending on the context (for instance, the elements' index in the case of List.Add()).

*Assert collection size: Assert the list size depending on whether a method is adding one item or multiple items.

*Assert the expected behavior: For instance, Assert that when you try to add an item already in the list, it won't increase the size of the list.


In terms of postconditions, you need to ensure that the new item has been added at the end of the list, and that no other item has been removed. It also needs to retain the current state of the list.
In your code, I believe you have missed the Assertions and Preconditions. Here is a sample format of how you can write a test:
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void AddItemContractor([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrayList, int item)
{
    //Assume Under Test
    Assume.IsTrue(arrayList != null, "ArrayList is null");
    AssumeContract.ObjectInitialState(PrePost, arrayList);

    //Act
    int ret = arrayList.Add(item);

    //Postconditions
    Postcondition.IsTrue(arrayList.Contains(item), "List contain item added");
    Postcondition.AreEqual(arrayList.Count, ret);
    Postcondition.IsTrue(arrayList.IndexOf(item) == ret, "Added item is at last index");

    //Observe Results
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Item added", item);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Return Value", ret);
}

In the code above:


*

*Assume Under Test: Assuming that ArrayList is not null

*Post Conditions: Ensure that the list will contain the added item, its count will be the return index, and the added item is at the last index.

*Observe Results: Visualize the item added and the return value result in a UI.


Hope this helps.
Cheers,
T.V.

