```
Note: If you plan to use the Pex API wrappers I'd advise you to go through their documentation first as this example just reflects the usage of some of the features available. Also please replace the "ArrayList" with the appropiate class name you're testing.

To run the fuzzing process, add the command `pex.exe YourTestFileNameHere.cs` on your project's root folder. This is assuming your Pex binaries are in your system's path. 

This will go through the test function and try a plethora of random inputs and outputs to ascertain the stability of your code and identify edge-cases. Be aware that it may take a while based on the complexity of your tests. I would recommend to run it with `/report:all` option so it provides a HTML report detailing each run. Enjoy fuzzing your code and learning together :)

Also, always remember that fuzzing does just a cursory quality check; automated tests with well defined inputs and outputs are needed for a more thorough check. The former are usually more effective in finding issues than the latter.

**NOTE**: Please replace "YourTestFileNameHere.cs" and replace "ArrayList" with the ArrayList class you intend to test. 

Hope this helps out :) 

**NOTE:** The examples provided here were written in C# and tested against .Net Framework version 4.5. 

**UPDATE:** Since Microsoft/Pex is now Microsoft/Pex-Framework, changes in the way to write tests and run them can be found at this repository.

Also, make sure you have enabled the Pex plugin in your VS and you've installed Pex from the Visual Studio marketplace. If not, you can download it from the link mentioned at the bottom of your installed Pex Tools tab in Visual Studio. 

Remember to understand and follow Microsoft Pex guidelines while writing your test scripts.
